WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.756 --> 00:00:19.699
19 Dear Daddy Longlegs, You never answered my question—are you bald?

2
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.981
It's a very important question because I'm trying to draw a picture of you.

3
00:00:27.863 --> 00:00:30.644
While I was drawing last night, Sallie came into my room.

4
00:00:33.752 --> 00:00:34.353
"Who's that?"

5
00:00:34.813 --> 00:00:35.293
she asked.

6
00:00:35.313 --> 00:00:39.157
"The kind old man who's sending me to college.

7
00:00:40.218 --> 00:00:42.840
It's difficult to draw him because I've never met him."

8
00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:47.023
"You've never met him, but he's sending you to college?

9
00:00:48.184 --> 00:00:49.906
That's very generous of him."

10
00:00:49.926 --> 00:00:51.927
"Yes, it is."

11
00:00:53.569 --> 00:00:58.213
I didn't look up because I didn't want her to ask any more questions about you—or me.

12
00:00:59.474 --> 00:01:01.776
So I concentrated on drawing your eyes.

13
00:01:03.104 --> 00:01:04.545
I decided to color them gray.

14
00:01:04.565 --> 00:01:08.866
"How do you know what color his eyes are?"

15
00:01:10.087 --> 00:01:11.828
Sallie stood behind me, watching.

16
00:01:11.848 --> 00:01:14.449
"I'm just guessing."

17
00:01:16.289 --> 00:01:17.830
I scribbled in your eyebrows.

18
00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.351
They stuck out like a porch roof.

19
00:01:21.912 --> 00:01:22.652
Sallie laughed.

20
00:01:23.793 --> 00:01:26.374
"Those are some eyebrows!"

21
00:01:27.714 --> 00:01:28.454
I laughed too.

22
00:01:28.475 --> 00:01:33.569
"I think they call them 'beetle brows' in novels."

23
00:01:33.589 --> 00:01:35.990
"I think they do."

24
00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:38.371
Sallie laughed again.

25
00:01:39.772 --> 00:01:41.532
But there was no laughter on your face.

26
00:01:42.373 --> 00:01:46.054
For your mouth, I drew a straight line that turned down at the corners.

27
00:01:46.074 --> 00:01:49.676
"He looks like a crabby old thing!"

28
00:01:50.116 --> 00:01:50.676
Sallie said.

29
00:01:50.696 --> 00:01:53.458
"Well, maybe he is.

30
00:01:53.558 --> 00:01:54.998
He never answers my letters."

31
00:01:55.018 --> 00:01:57.419
"That's rude!"

32
00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:58.360
Sallie said.

33
00:01:59.648 --> 00:02:01.989
"He's a kind but rude old man."

34
00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:05.650
Until this point I'd been drawing quickly.

35
00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:09.231
But when I reached the top of your head, I got stuck.

36
00:02:09.251 --> 00:02:14.652
"I can't decide whether he has white or black hair.

37
00:02:15.993 --> 00:02:18.973
Or maybe he has gray hair mixed with dark hair.

38
00:02:20.734 --> 00:02:21.534
What's that called?

39
00:02:23.115 --> 00:02:23.735
Oh, I know.

40
00:02:24.075 --> 00:02:24.795
Salt and pepper."

41
00:02:26.636 --> 00:02:27.916
"Or maybe he's bald!"

42
00:02:28.316 --> 00:02:28.916
Sallie said.

43
00:02:28.937 --> 00:02:31.718
"That's another possibility."

44
00:02:33.359 --> 00:02:34.400
I put down my pencil.

45
00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:39.123
"I have to stop drawing until I figure out what kind of hair he has."

46
00:02:39.143 --> 00:02:41.744
"How are you going to find that out?"

47
00:02:42.024 --> 00:02:42.665
Sallie asked.

48
00:02:42.685 --> 00:02:45.066
"I'll write him a letter, of course."

49
00:02:46.467 --> 00:02:49.209
So here I am writing you another letter about your hair.

50
00:02:50.469 --> 00:02:54.812
And now, it's not only me who wants to know its color; Sallie wants to know too.

51
00:02:56.056 --> 00:02:57.798
There was something else Sallie wanted to know.

52
00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:00.400
But it was about me, not you.

53
00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:05.444
"Every night you put a Do Not Disturb sign on your door," she said.

54
00:03:05.464 --> 00:03:09.107
"I'm lucky you were free tonight.

55
00:03:10.048 --> 00:03:12.029
What are you so busy doing in here?"

56
00:03:12.049 --> 00:03:15.632
"Oh, just studying."

57
00:03:18.534 --> 00:03:20.916
I rolled up my drawing and tucked it in a desk drawer.

58
00:03:20.936 --> 00:03:23.999
"You must really like to study!"

59
00:03:24.439 --> 00:03:25.000
Sallie said.

60
00:03:25.984 --> 00:03:27.906
The truth is, I'm not studying.

61
00:03:29.347 --> 00:03:32.910
I never study at night, no matter how many essays are due the next day.

62
00:03:34.251 --> 00:03:35.873
Instead I just read.

63
00:03:37.454 --> 00:03:42.398
There are so many novels that I've never read, and so many things that I don't know.

64
00:03:43.559 --> 00:03:48.123
The other girls seem to know everything while I have 18 blank years behind me.

65
00:03:50.505 --> 00:03:53.267
I'd never read Oliver Twist or Jane Eyre.

66
00:03:55.912 --> 00:04:00.835
I didn't know that King Henry VIII was married more than once or that Shelley was a poet.

67
00:04:04.817 --> 00:04:06.658
I didn't know that R.L.S.

68
00:04:06.998 --> 00:04:10.800
stood for Robert Louis Stevenson or that he wrote Treasure Island.

69
00:04:12.381 --> 00:04:14.262
I'd never seen a picture of the Mona Lisa.

70
00:04:16.403 --> 00:04:20.846
And you probably won't believe this, but I'd never heard of Sherlock Holmes either!

71
00:04:22.587 --> 00:04:25.008
Now I know all these things and a lot more.

72
00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.641
But you can see how I needed to catch up.

73
00:04:28.181 --> 00:04:28.841
And it's fun!

74
00:04:31.301 --> 00:04:33.242
All day I look forward to the evening.

75
00:04:35.543 --> 00:04:39.284
Then I put on my red bathrobe, sit on my couch, and read.

76
00:04:40.884 --> 00:04:41.884
One book isn't enough.

77
00:04:43.045 --> 00:04:44.725
I usually have four going at once.

78
00:04:45.965 --> 00:04:47.446
Right now I'm reading Little Women.

79
00:04:48.646 --> 00:04:53.687
When I discovered I was the only girl in the dorm who didn't know that book, I said

80
00:04:53.747 --> 00:04:59.822
nothing. I just went and bought it with $1.12 of my allowance.

81
00:05:01.563 --> 00:05:03.364
The Christmas holidays begin next week.

82
00:05:06.566 --> 00:05:09.067
Most of the girls are starting to pack their trunks to go home.

83
00:05:11.388 --> 00:05:16.051
The dormitory hallways are so full of trunks that you can hardly get past them.

84
00:05:18.292 --> 00:05:20.073
Everyone is bubbling over with excitement.

85
00:05:21.113 --> 00:05:22.434
Nobody is studying very much.

86
00:05:23.975 --> 00:05:27.389
I'm going to have a wonderful time, even though I'll be staying here.

87
00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:32.071
There's another freshman who isn't going home.

88
00:05:34.331 --> 00:05:36.973
She's from Texas, so it's too far to travel.

89
00:05:38.073 --> 00:05:39.614
We're planning to take long walks.

90
00:05:41.554 --> 00:05:44.095
And if there's any ice, we want to learn to skate.

91
00:05:47.097 --> 00:05:52.399
Then there's the whole library full of books—and three weeks with nothing to do but read!

92
00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:54.940
Good-bye, Daddy.

93
00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:57.762
I hope that you're feeling as happy as I am.

94
00:05:58.863 --> 00:06:03.387
Yours truly, Judy P.S.

95
00:06:03.967 --> 00:06:05.508
Don't forget to answer my question.

96
00:06:06.049 --> 00:06:09.772
If you don't want to answer, have your secretary send me a telegram.

97
00:06:11.313 --> 00:06:12.914
He can just say Mr.

98
00:06:12.995 --> 00:06:14.216
Smith is quite bald.

99
00:06:14.716 --> 00:06:15.597
Or Mr.

100
00:06:15.657 --> 00:06:16.738
Smith is not bald.

101
00:06:17.238 --> 00:06:17.839
Or Mr.

102
00:06:17.899 --> 00:06:18.799
Smith has white hair.

103
00:06:20.501 --> 00:06:24.044
And you can deduct the 25 cents for the telegram from my allowance.

104
00:06:26.297 --> 00:06:28.826
Good-bye until January—and have a merry Christmas!
